--TEST--
`Core\Response::bytes` sends its buffer verbatim, including octets no text-shaped body member could carry -- `rule:security/response-body-is-one-typed-member`, `rule:types/bytes`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:security/response-body-is-one-typed-member`'s fourth row is the member for a body that is not text, so the
// edge worth pinning is the one `text` cannot reach: a buffer holding a NUL,
// control bytes and a byte that is not valid UTF-8 goes out unchanged. The
// parent reads each body back with `Core\Test::request`, whose response keeps
// the body as bytes. A capture cannot read it: it throws for bytes that are
// not UTF-8, and it replaces control bytes.
if (Core\Env::context() == Core\Env::Context::Request) {
    var $path = Core\Request::path();
    if ($path == "/signature") {
        Core\Response::bytes(Core\Encoding::fromHex("504e470d0a1a0a00"), "image/png");
    }
    if ($path == "/octets") {
        Core\Response::bytes(Core\Encoding::fromHex("504e470d0a1a0a00ff"), "image/png");
    }
    if ($path == "/empty") {
        Core\Response::bytes("" as bytes, "application/octet-stream");
    }
    if ($path == "/csv") {
        Core\Response::bytes("id,name" as bytes, "text/csv; charset=utf-8; header=present");
    }
} else {
    // The NUL and the control bytes are valid UTF-8, so `body()` returns them,
    // and the hex is the hex that was sent.
    var $signature = Core\Test::request(Core\Http\Method::Get, "/signature");
    echo Core\Encoding::toHex($signature->body() as bytes), "\n";

    // The same octets and then `FF`. `body()` throws at byte 8, so the first
    // eight octets arrived unchanged and `FF` arrived as itself. A replacement
    // character in its place is valid UTF-8, and `body()` would return it.
    var $octets = Core\Test::request(Core\Http\Method::Get, "/octets");
    try {
        echo $octets->body(), "\n";
    } catch (RuntimeError $notText) {
        echo $octets->status(), " ", $notText->message, "\n";
    }

    // An empty body is a body: nothing is written, and nothing throws.
    var $empty = Core\Test::request(Core\Http\Method::Get, "/empty");
    echo "[", $empty->body(), "] ", $empty->header("Content-Type") ?? "none", "\n";

    // A media type with parameters is one string, and it is declared as given.
    var $csv = Core\Test::request(Core\Http\Method::Get, "/csv");
    echo $csv->header("Content-Type") ?? "none", " ", $csv->body(), "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
504e470d0a1a0a00
200 Core\Test\Response::body(): the response body is not valid UTF-8 at byte 8, so it is not a string. Use `status()` and `header()` to test this response.
[] application/octet-stream
text/csv; charset=utf-8; header=present id,name
